{
  "title": "Distance at Constant Speed",
  "scope": "Calculating distance using constant speed and time, and analyzing proportional scaling relationships at an introductory level.",
  "objectives": [
    {
      "id": "o1",
      "label": "Calculate distance from speed and time",
      "criterion": "The learner correctly applies the formula $\\text{distance} = \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time}$ to compute distance when given speed and time values with matching units.",
      "sourceIds": ["s1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "o2",
      "label": "Analyze proportional scaling effects",
      "criterion": "The learner explains and demonstrates how doubling the time or doubling the speed results in a proportional doubling of the calculated distance.",
      "sourceIds": ["s1"]
    }
  ],
  "knowledge": "At a constant speed, the total distance traveled is directly proportional to the product of the speed and the elapsed time, expressed as $\\text{distance} = \\text{speed} \\times \\text{time}$. This fundamental relationship means that if the speed remains unchanged, increasing the duration of travel increases the distance linearly. Similarly, traveling at a higher speed for the same duration covers proportionally more ground. Dimensional consistency is essential: when speed is measured in metres per second (\\pu{m/s}) and time in seconds (\\pu{s}), their product yields distance in metres (\\pu{m}). Proportional scaling shows that doubling either the time or the speed scales the resulting distance by a factor of two.",
  "concerns": [],
  "misconception": "Doubling both the speed and the time at the same time will only double the distance.",
  "misconceptionCorrection": "When you double both speed and time simultaneously, the distance actually quadruples because both factors increase by a factor of two, multiplying the overall effect ($2 \\times 2 = 4$).",
  "opening": "I keep seeing problems about distance, speed, and time, but I am not entirely sure how they relate. Could you teach me how to calculate distance when speed is constant?",
  "transfer": {
    "question": "A rover on a distant plain travels at a constant speed of \\pu{4 m/s} for a duration of \\pu{25 s}. If the operating engineers decide to double both the speed and the time of travel, what will the new total distance be?",
    "expected": "To find the new distance, first determine the new speed by doubling the original \\pu{4 m/s} to get \\pu{8 m/s}. Next, determine the new time by doubling the original \\pu{25 s} to get \\pu{50 s}. Multiplying the new speed by the new time gives the total distance: \\pu{8 m/s} \\times \\pu{50 s} = \\pu{400 m}. Alternatively, since doubling both speed and time multiplies the original distance by four ($2 \\times 2 = 4$), the original distance of \\pu{100 m} multiplied by four also yields \\pu{400 m}.",
    "criteria": [
      "Calculates the new speed and new time correctly after doubling.",
      "Multiplies the new speed by the new time to find the correct total distance.",
      "Explains that doubling both variables results in a fourfold increase in distance."
    ],
    "numeric": {
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "new_distance",
          "label": "New Distance",
          "unit": "m",
          "expected": 400,
          "tolerance": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "inputs": [
        {
          "id": "speed",
          "value": 4
        },
        {
          "id": "time",
          "value": 25
        }
      ],
      "code": "function calculate(inputs) { const s = inputs.speed * 2; const t = inputs.time * 2; const d = s * t; return { metrics: { new_distance: d } }; }"
    }
  },
  "board": {
    "title": "Constant Speed Explorer",
    "instruction": "Adjust the speed and time sliders to see how the distance changes, and observe how proportional scaling affects the result.",
    "assumptions": "Speed remains strictly constant throughout the entire travel duration.",
    "state": [
      {
        "id": "speed",
        "label": "Speed",
        "unit": "m/s",
        "type": "number",
        "initial": 5,
        "min": 1,
        "max": 20,
        "step": 1,
        "options": []
      },
      {
        "id": "time",
        "label": "Time",
        "unit": "s",
        "type": "number",
        "initial": 10,
        "min": 1,
        "max": 60,
        "step": 1,
        "options": []
      }
    ],
    "derived": [
      {
        "id": "distance",
        "label": "Distance",
        "unit": "m"
      }
    ],
    "code": "function calculate(inputs) { const dist = inputs.speed * inputs.time; return { metrics: { distance: dist } }; }",
